<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Buchhorn</h1><p class="st-meta">1275 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Buchhorn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny imperial city on the north shore of Lake Constance that was merged with a neighbouring priory to form the town of Friedrichshafen in 1811.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Buchhorn grew on the north shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> as a fishing and market settlement under the counts of Buchhorn and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>. After the fall of the Hohenstaufen it was recognised as an imperial city by Rudolf I around 1275.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Buchhorn was one of the smallest imperial cities, with a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and a tiny territory. It lived from fishing, lake shipping and trade across the lake to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>, and its market served the surrounding countryside. It remained Catholic and depended on alliances with other Swabian cities and on Austrian goodwill.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> annexed Buchhorn in 1802 and passed it to Württemberg in 1810. In 1811 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg">King Frederick I of Württemberg</a> merged it with the neighbouring Hofen priory to create Friedrichshafen, later the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin">Zeppelin</a> airships.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1275 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.07%;width:10.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1275 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buchhorn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Buchhorn, Friedrichshafen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1538&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-buchhorn" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Buchhorn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4901 508 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Buchhorn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5221 764L5229 694M5221 764L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 764h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5221 764h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Buchhorn. <a href="/atlas?year=1538&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-buchhorn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
